p.src:
Assume the energy eigenvalue problem has been solved. Expand an arbitrary state in the fixed energy basis: $\ket{\psi(t)}=\sum_j\alpha_j(t)\ket{E_j}.$ The basis vectors do not change in this description; all time dependence lies in the coefficients. Substituting Equation  into Equation  gives $\begin{aligned}
\sum_j\dot\alpha_j(t)\ket{E_j}
&=-i\sum_j\alpha_j(t)H\ket{E_j}\\
&=-i\sum_j E_j\alpha_j(t)\ket{E_j}.\end{aligned}$ Uniqueness of the expansion in a basis implies, for each fixed $j$, $\dot\alpha_j(t)=-iE_j\alpha_j(t),
\qquad\text{no sum on \(j\)}.$ The solution is $\alpha_j(t)=\alpha_j(0)e^{-iE_jt}.$

